{"id":"6a42f64a-cce9-4077-b7cf-e56c1be08412","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885366,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    trajectory = []\\n    allowed_next = {\\n        None: ['Thought'],\\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\\n        'Final': []\\n    }\\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\\n        line = raw_line.strip()\\n        if not line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\\n            break\\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\\n        if len(parts) != 2:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\\n            break\\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n        prev_type = prefix\\n    else:\\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\\n            valid = False\\n            if trajectory:\\n                error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n            else:\\n                error_line = None\\n    return {\\n        'valid': valid,\\n        'error_line': error_line,\\n        'trajectory': trajectory\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task AGENT-RA-01. The input case is a payload object with fields \"lines\" (list of strings) and possibly \"_case_nonce\". From the fixture, the lines are like \"Thought: ...\", \"Action: ...\", \"Observation: ...\", \"Final: ...\". The task is likely to validate a trajectory of agent steps. The output must follow the schema: valid boolean, error_line integer or null, trajectory array of objects with type and content strings.\n\nThe task ID \"AGENT-RA-01\" suggests \"agent reasoning action\" validation. Common pattern: we need to parse lines into a sequence of steps: Thought, Action, Observation, Final? Possibly lines represent alternating roles. The expected pattern: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> Thought -> ... -> Final. The error_line should indicate the line index (0-based?) where something goes wrong. If valid, error_line is null.\n\nFrom the fixture, all three cases have the same lines: \n0: \"Thought: inspect\"\n1: \"Action: lookup\"\n2: \"Observation: found\"\n3: \"Thought: answer\"\n4: \"Final: done\"\n\nThat seems valid. So for those, valid=true, error_line=null, trajectory should contain the steps as objects.\n\nWe need to infer the rules. Probably each line must match a regex: ^(Thought|Action|Observation|Final): .*$ . And the sequence must follow the pattern: must start with Thought, then can alternate Action->Observation? Actually typical ReAct: Thought, Action, Observation, Thought, Action, Observation, ... until Final. The pattern: after Thought, must be Action; after Action, must be Observation; after Observation, must be Thought or Final? But the observation is after action. Then after observation, either Thought or Final (if done). So sequence: Thought -> Action -> Observation -> (Thought or Final) -> etc. And Final must be the last line. Also, no duplicate types? Actually it's fine.\n\nAlso, the content after colon is free text. That's the \"content\" field. The \"type\" is the keyword before colon.\n\nSo algorithm:\n- Iterate over lines with index.\n- Parse each line: split on first colon. The part before colon must be one of: \"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\". If not, invalid.\n- Then check sequence expectation:\n  - Expect first line type is \"Thought\".\n  - After \"Thought\", expect \"Action\".\n  - After \"Action\", expect \"Observation\".\n  - After \"Observation\", expect \"Thought\" or \"Final\".\n  - After \"Final\", nothing else (end).\n- If any mismatch, set valid=false, error_line = index of the line that broke the rule.\n- If all lines parsed and sequence ends with Final, valid=true, error_line=null.\n\nAlso, handle empty lines? Probably not, as per fixtures.\n\nReturn trajectory list of dicts with type and content (trimmed? just the part after colon, maybe strip whitespace). Ensure order.\n\nConstraints: use only standard library, no external libs.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) that returns a dict with keys valid, error_line, trajectory.\n\nEdge cases: lines may be empty, but we'll treat as invalid. Also, if final line is missing, invalid.\n\nThe trajectory should include all lines that are valid? Including invalid lines? Probably include all lines in order, even if invalid? The schema says trajectory items have type and content. The error_line indicates where error occurred. The trajectory likely includes the lines up to that point? Or all lines? The examples don't show invalid cases. We should include all lines that we parsed successfully? Since we need to return a trajectory regardless of validity? The requirement: \"Return the result for that payload.\" The trajectory is required, so we should populate it with the parsed steps for all lines, even if invalid? Or only for valid ones? Typically, in agent-RA tasks, the trajectory includes the steps as parsed, and error_line points to the first invalid step. So we can parse all lines and still include them in trajectory, but error_line indicates which index is problematic. For invalid lines, we could still include their parsed type and content? The line may not match the pattern. If a line doesn't match the pattern (no colon or wrong keyword), then we cannot parse type/content. In that case, what to include? The schema doesn't define error-handling for trajectory. We could include a dummy or skip? Better to include the raw line as type? But the spec says content is string, type is string. For invalid line, we could set type to \"invalid\" or something? I think the test cases likely only have valid lines. But to be safe, we can treat any line that does not match the pattern as error_line, and then we could either still include it with type and content from the line? But we don't have colon split. We could set type to the whole line and content empty? That might not be expected.\n\nGiven the fixture all valid, we'll assume all lines follow pattern. For robustness, if a line doesn't match, we set valid=false, error_line=index, and still add to trajectory with type as the part before colon if exists else \"unknown\", content as rest after colon if exists else line. But the schema allows any string, so it's okay.\n\nWe'll implement regex or simple split.\n\nImplementation plan:\n1. Define allowed_types = {\"Thought\", \"Action\", \"Observation\", \"Final\"}\n2. Initialize trajectory = [], error_line = None, valid = True.\n3. Define expected_next = None or a function that returns next expected type based on previous.\n   Use a state machine: start state expects \"Thought\".\n   After \"Thought\", expect \"Action\".\n   After \"Action\", expect \"Observation\".\n   After \"Observation\", expect either \"Thought\" or \"Final\".\n   After \"Final\", expect nothing; if more lines, error.\n4. Iterate lines with enumerate index i, line.\n   - strip whitespace? The lines in fixture have no leading/trailing spaces. We'll strip to be safe.\n   - if not line: treat as invalid? Or skip? Better to treat as invalid line.\n   - Split on first colon using line.split(\":\", 1). If less than 2 parts, invalid (no colon).\n   - prefix = part0.strip(), suffix = part1.strip().\n   - if prefix not in allowed_types: invalid.\n   - Check if prefix matches expected_next (based on state). If not, error.\n   - If prefix == \"Final\" and state expecting Final (i.e., after Observation), then we set state to \"done\", no further lines expected.\n   - Add dict type=prefix, content=suffix to trajectory.\n   - Update state.\n5. After loop, if state is not \"done\" (i.e., we didn't end with Final or we have incomplete sequence), set valid=False and error_line to appropriate index? The error_line should be the index of the last line? Or the missing line? Better to set error_line to len(lines)-1 if last line is not Final? Or maybe null? According to schema, error_line can be integer or null. For invalid due to missing final, we could set error_line to the last line index (i.e., the last line is not final, so it's invalid). Or set to null? The specification doesn't detail. But typical tasks set error_line to the index where the invalid step occurs. If the sequence is incomplete (e.g., ends with Observation), then the last line is valid but the next step is missing, so error_line should be the index of the next expected line? But that line doesn't exist. Could set error_line to null and valid=False? But the schema allows error_line to be integer; possibly they expect the line index where the error is detected. In case of missing final, we might set error_line to None? The fixture doesn't show. I'll assume that if the sequence is incomplete (never seen Final), then error_line = len(lines) (i.e., the index where a line should be) or -1? Better to set it to the index of the last line because that line is not final, so the error is that the last line is not final. Or set to null? The schema doesn't require error_line to be non-null when valid=False. So we can set it to the index of the line that violates the expected sequence. If the last line is valid but the next is missing, then error_line should be the last line index (since it should have been followed by Final). That seems reasonable.\n\nWe'll implement: After loop, if state != \"done\": valid=False, and if trajectory non-empty, error_line = len(lines)-1 (last line index) because that line should have been followed by something else. If no lines at all, error_line = 0? But there would be no trajectory. We'll handle empty array: valid=False, error_line = 0? Or null? Better to set error_line = 0 and add dummy? Not needed. We'll just set error_line = 0 if no lines, else len(lines)-1.\n\nBut careful: If the first line is not Thought, we set error_line to that index immediately. So works.\n\nReturn dict.\n\nTesting with fixture: all lines follow pattern and end with Final, so valid=True, error_line=null, trajectory length 5.\n\nCase: two thoughts? The pattern allows Thought -> Action -> Observation -> Thought -> Action -> Observation -> ... -> Final. So multiple cycles are fine as long as correct alternation.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNow produce Python code as a JSON string under \"implementation\". Must escape properly.\n\nThe entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". So we'll output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    ...\"}\n\nEnsure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write the function.\n\nLet me write in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    trajectory = []\n    # state machine for expected next type\n    # states: None (start), 'Thought', 'Action', 'Observation', 'Done'\n    expected = 'Thought'  # initially expect Thought\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\n        line = raw_line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            # still add? maybe not, but if we add, need type content. We'll break or continue? For simplicity, treat as invalid line, set error and break? But we need to continue to build trajectory? Usually we stop at first error. Let's stop.\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})  # maybe\n            break\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\n            break\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\n        if prefix not in ['Thought','Action','Observation','Final']:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})  # still add\n            break\n        # check expected\n        if prefix != expected:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n            break\n        # update expected\n        if prefix == 'Thought':\n            expected = 'Action'\n        elif prefix == 'Action':\n            expected = 'Observation'\n        elif prefix == 'Observation':\n            expected = 'Thought'  # but can also be Final? Actually after Observation, next expected can be Thought or Final. We'll handle after checking allowed.\n        elif prefix == 'Final':\n            expected = 'Done'  # no more lines\n        # Now check if after Observation, prefix == 'Observation', we expect either Thought or Final. But we already checked prefix == expected, which is 'Thought'. So we need to allow Final as well. So we need to adjust: after Observation, expected can be either Thought or Final. So we need a set of expected types. So we can have a set instead of single.\n        # Let's redesign state machine more flexibly.\n    # we'll use a state variable that is a list/set of allowed next types.\n    # Actually we can have a dictionary mapping current type to next allowed types.\n    allowed_next = {\n        None: ['Thought'],\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\n        'Final': []  # no more\n    }\n    # We'll track previous type, starting with None.\n    # Then check if line's type in allowed_next[prev_type].\n    # This is cleaner.\n\nLet's rewrite:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    trajectory = []\n    allowed_next = {\n        None: ['Thought'],\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\n        'Final': []  # no more allowed\n    }\n    prev_type = None\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\n        line = raw_line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\n            break\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\n            break\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n            break\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n            break\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n        prev_type = prefix\n    else:\n        # loop completed without break\n        # check if final state is Final\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\n            valid = False\n            # set error_line to the index of the last line? Or last line index? Since last line didn't complete, we'll set error_line to len(lines)-1 if lines else 0\n            error_line = len(lines)-1 if lines else 0\n    return {\n        'valid': valid,\n        'error_line': error_line,\n        'trajectory': trajectory\n    }\n\nBut note: In the loop, if we break due to invalid, we might not have added the offending line? We did add before break. That's fine. However, after break, we don't want to continue. The else clause runs only if loop didn't break, so it handles the no-error case. Good.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where there are no lines: then loop not entered, prev_type remains None, we enter else, prev_type != 'Final', so valid=False, error_line=0 (or None? I set 0). But maybe error_line should be null? They might expect null if empty list? But empty list is invalid. I'll set error_line = len(lines)-1 which is -1? Actually if lines is empty, len(lines)-1 = -1. That's not a valid index. Better to set error_line = None if no lines? But the output schema requires integer or null. None is allowed. So we can set error_line = None if lines empty. However, the spec might expect integer indexing valid lines. To be safe, set error_line = None if no lines at all and invalid because no steps. Or set to -1? But -1 is an integer, could be misinterpreted. I'll use None.\n\nModify: after loop, if not trajectory (i.e., no lines processed), then error_line = None. Else if invalid due to incomplete sequence, set error_line to len(lines)-1 (last line index) because that line is not Final.\n\nBut if the first line is invalid, we break and error_line set to that index. Good.\n\nNow test with the given fixture. For each case, lines list same.\n\nPrev_type start None -> first line 'Thought' is allowed -> add, prev_type='Thought'. Second line 'Action' is allowed for prev='Thought' -> add. Third 'Observation' allowed. Fourth 'Thought' allowed (since prev='Observation' allows 'Thought'). Fifth 'Final' allowed (since prev='Observation' allows 'Final'). After loop, prev_type='Final', so valid=True, error_line=None. trajectory length 5. Good.\n\nNow need to ensure we don't include _case_nonce in output. Only use 'lines' key.\n\nAlso, case may have other keys; ignore.\n\nReturn dict must be JSON serializable.\n\nWrite final code.\n\nEdge: The lines might have trailing spaces; we strip.\n\nI think this implementation covers the pattern.\n\nNow produce the JSON string.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    trajectory = []\\n    allowed_next = {\\n        None: ['Thought'],\\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\\n        'Final': []\\n    }\\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\\n        line = raw_line.strip()\\n        if not line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\\n            break\\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\\n        if len(parts) != 2:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\\n            break\\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n        prev_type = prefix\\n    else:\\n        # loop completed without break\\n        # check if final step is Final\\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\\n            valid = False\\n            if trajectory:\\n                error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n            else:\\n                error_line = None\\n    return {\\n        'valid': valid,\\n        'error_line': error_line,\\n        'trajectory': trajectory\\n    }\"\n}\n\nMake sure to escape backslashes, quotes, newlines. In the JSON string, we need to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, double quotes as \\\". Let's verify the string.\n\nWe'll produce the final JSON.\n\nBut ensure that the string does not contain any unescaped quotes inside the function definition. We use single quotes for string literals in Python, so no double quotes inside the Python code, except in dict keys. The dictionary keys like 'lines' are single quotes, fine. But we have string literals with double quotes? Actually we have {\"type\": \"invalid\", \"content\": line} - that uses double quotes? No, we wrote {'type': 'invalid', 'content': line} using single quotes for the keys and double quotes? Wait in Python we used single quotes for dict keys? In the code I wrote: trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''} - that's single quotes. So no double quotes inside the Python code that would need escaping. Good.\n\nBut the case input from fixture uses double quotes, but that's irrelevant. Our function doesn't reference that.\n\nSo the implementation string is safe.\n\nWe need to embed it as a JSON string value. So we need to escape backslashes and newlines. For readability, we can replace newlines with \\n. But we must be careful with indentation spaces. The code should be a single line string? JSON allows newlines in strings? Actually JSON strings can contain literal newlines? They must be escaped as \\n. So we need to replace each newline with \\n. Also, backslashes: we don't have any in the code except in the allowed_next dict we have 'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'] no backslash. So fine.\n\nWe'll write the string with \\n for newlines.\n\nAlternatively, we can use a raw string in the JSON? No, we must provide valid JSON.\n\nI'll manually construct the JSON string with escaped newlines.\n\nWe'll write the function as a single line with \\n.\n\nLet's compose the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    trajectory = []\n    allowed_next = {\n        None: ['Thought'],\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\n        'Final': []\n    }\n    prev_type = None\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\n        line = raw_line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\n            break\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\n            break\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n            break\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\n            valid = False\n            error_line = i\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n            break\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\n        prev_type = prefix\n    else:\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\n            valid = False\n            if trajectory:\n                error_line = len(lines) - 1\n            else:\n                error_line = None\n    return {\n        'valid': valid,\n        'error_line': error_line,\n        'trajectory': trajectory\n    }\n\nNow convert to string with \\n. I'll write it as a Python multiline string and then replace newlines manually for the JSON.\n\nBetter to produce the JSON programmatically? But I must write it manually.\n\nI'll write the string like:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    trajectory = []\\n    allowed_next = {\\n        None: ['Thought'],\\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\\n        'Final': []\\n    }\\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\\n        line = raw_line.strip()\\n        if not line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\\n            break\\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\\n        if len(parts) != 2:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\\n            break\\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n        prev_type = prefix\\n    else:\\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\\n            valid = False\\n            if trajectory:\\n                error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n            else:\\n                error_line = None\\n    return {\\n        'valid': valid,\\n        'error_line': error_line,\\n        'trajectory': trajectory\\n    }\"\n\nNote that the allowed_next dictionary has None key. In Python, it's fine. Also, the 'Observation' key has list of two strings. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to ensure that the JSON string is properly escaped: backslashes before newlines are already there. However, inside the string, we have double quotes? None. Single quotes are fine, but JSON strings must not contain unescaped double quotes. There are no double quotes. Also, we have backslashes only before n? Actually we have backslashes that are part of the escape sequences in the JSON string itself. So in the JSON text, we need to write the string as: \"def solve(case):\\\\n    lines ...\" because in JSON, backslash is escape character, so we need to double-escape: for a literal backslash in the Python code, we need to represent it as \\\\ in JSON. But here the Python code has no backslashes except for newlines? Actually the Python code uses \\n inside the string? No, we are writing the Python code as a literal string; the Python code includes backslashes only in string literals? The code does not contain backslash characters. In the implementation string, we have '\\n' sequences which are not literal backslash-n in the Python source; they are the newline character in the JSON string representation. So in the JSON, we need to put \\n for newline, and that's a single backslash followed by n in the JSON string. That's correct: the JSON string value should contain the escape sequence \\n to represent a newline character. So we write \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = ...\" and within the JSON, the backslash is the start of an escape. That's fine. There is no need to double-escape because we are literally writing the \\n escape in the JSON string.\n\nBut careful: When we write the JSON, the characters we type include a backslash and then an 'n'. That is valid JSON for a newline. So we don't need to double it. However, if the Python code itself contained a literal backslash (e.g., for regex), we would need to escape that. But it doesn't.\n\nThus the implementation string as shown is fine; we just need to embed it in a JSON object.\n\nNow produce the final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    lines = case.get('lines', [])\\n    valid = True\\n    error_line = None\\n    trajectory = []\\n    allowed_next = {\\n        None: ['Thought'],\\n        'Thought': ['Action'],\\n        'Action': ['Observation'],\\n        'Observation': ['Thought', 'Final'],\\n        'Final': []\\n    }\\n    prev_type = None\\n    for i, raw_line in enumerate(lines):\\n        line = raw_line.strip()\\n        if not line:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': ''})\\n            break\\n        parts = line.split(':', 1)\\n        if len(parts) != 2:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'invalid', 'content': line})\\n            break\\n        prefix = parts[0].strip()\\n        suffix = parts[1].strip()\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next:\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        if prefix not in allowed_next.get(prev_type, []):\\n            valid = False\\n            error_line = i\\n            trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n            break\\n        trajectory.append({'type': prefix, 'content': suffix})\\n        prev_type = prefix\\n    else:\\n        if prev_type != 'Final':\\n            valid = False\\n            if trajectory:\\n                error_line = len(lines) - 1\\n            else:\\n                error_line = None\\n    return {\\n        'valid': valid,\\n        'error_line': error_line,\\n        'trajectory': trajectory\\n    }\"\n}\n\nBut note that there are multiple lines; we have to ensure that the entire string is on one line? JSON allows multi-line strings? The JSON spec says a string can contain any Unicode character except control characters; newlines are control characters and must be escaped as \\n. So we cannot have literal newlines in the JSON string; we must use \\n. So our representation with actual line breaks in the JSON would be invalid. We must place the entire string on one line, using \\n for newlines. So we need to write the string without breaking lines, but with the \\n escape sequences.\n\nSo I'll create the string without